window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-e75b0b93211820a9"]={"key":"ny_b1-e75b0b93211820a9","group":"ny_b1","uid":"e75b0b93211820a9","rank":47,"title":"asthma guidance magazine feature 001","occupation":"nurse_practitioners","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You are Bartholomew Ives, the nurse practitioner who runs the pediatric asthma clinic at Rushmere Trust. Naomi Fenwick, the editor of our quarterly magazine, has 1,400 words open in the spring issue, which reaches about 90,000 households, and wants the lead feature on why families are being told to move away from a rescue-only inhaler regimen that many parents have used for twenty years. Please write a publishable feature for her, not a patient handout or lecture summary, and save it as `rushmere_asthma_inhaler_feature.docx` in the top level of the working directory. Write for an intelligent general audience, with a warm, calm magazine voice that respects people who were taught the older approach and does not imply that their previous clinicians were careless. The piece needs a strong opening, a sustained central argument, clear transitions, and an ending that feels like the end of an article rather than a list of next steps. Use the magazine's attribution convention: put numbered superscript citations in the prose and finish with a compact Sources note listing each cited source by organization or author, title, and year; cite recognized asthma guidance and evidence rather than inventing local statistics or patient stories. Families may have heard “stop using the blue inhaler” as an absolute instruction, while some cannot obtain, tolerate, correctly use, or promptly replace the anti-inflammatory treatment being recommended, and some have symptoms whose diagnosis or severity has not been firmly established. The article must make those situations understandable without becoming individualized prescribing advice. Keep the finished feature between 1,300 and 1,400 words inclusive, including the Sources note, and do not add a title page, author biography, editor's note, pull quotes, or a reference file. Do not name or describe any individual patient, and do not claim that one inhaler plan is safe for every child; the clinical reviewer needs language that distinguishes routine prevention, symptom relief, and urgent escalation.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `rushmere_asthma_inhaler_feature.docx` — A publishable magazine feature explaining the move away from rescue-only asthma treatment, with numbered superscript citations and a compact Sources note. (Use exactly the specified filename and place it at the top level of the working directory.; The feature, including the Sources note, must be 1,300 to 1,400 words inclusive.; Do not include a title page, author biography, editor's note, pull quotes, patient-identifying details, or individualized prescribing instructions.; Use numbered superscript citations in the prose and a Sources note identifying cited sources by organization or author, title, and year.; The prose must be suitable for publication in a general-interest health magazine and must not read like a clinical handout.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/rushmere_asthma_inhaler_feature.docx","bytes":41009,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/1b6cc3a7f57bf5476c9c236fa26227114123c2bfbe07f6bda38ca800e116bc87.docx","sha256":"1b6cc3a7f57bf5476c9c236fa26227114123c2bfbe07f6bda38ca800e116bc87","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"The blue inhaler is not the villain\nWhy asthma care is changing, and what that change really asks of families\nFor many parents, the blue inhaler is a memory as much as a medicine: quick relief when a child began to wheeze. Being told to move away from a “rescue-only” routine can sound like an instruction to remove a treatment that has worked. The blue inhaler still has an important job. What has changed is our understanding of what it cannot do alone.\nAsthma is a condition in which the airways—the tubes carrying air into the lungs—can become inflamed and narrow. The degree of narrowing varies: a child may be comfortable in the morning and cough or struggle during a viral infection, exercise or exposure to smoke. A short-acting bronchodilator, often called a reliever, relaxes airway muscle and can make breathing easier within minutes. That relief is real, but it does not settle the underlying inflammation. Feeling better now is not the same as lowering the chance of a serious flare later.\nFor years, many care pathways treated the reliever as the centre of the plan, adding a daily “controller” when symptoms became persistent. Families followed available advice, and clinicians worked with the evidence and inhalers of their time. Newer evidence and guidance have changed the balance. The Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA) now advises against relying on a short-acting bronchodilator alone for most people with asthma, because frequent use can leave inflammation and future risk unaddressed.¹ This is an evolution in risk management, not proof that earlier doctors or parents were careless.\nWhy does frequency matter? A reliever can appear to work wonderfully: each puff opens the airways and a child gets back to play. Yet needing it repeatedly, waking at night, or reaching for it before ordinary activities can warn that asthma is not well controlled. It can also signal that triggers, technique, diagnosis or access need a fresh look. Immediate response should not alone declare the condition safe. Symptom improvement may coexist with inflammation that increases the risk of an exacerbation—symptoms substantially worse and needing urgent assessment.\nThe newer approach pairs relief with protection. Inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) are anti-inflammatory medicines: used as prescribed, they reduce swelling and sensitivity in the airways and lower the likelihood of severe attacks for many people. Some regimens put an anti-inflammatory medicine in the same inhaler used for symptoms; others use a separate preventer. The exact choice depends on age, pattern of symptoms, previous exacerbations, confirmed diagnosis, other health conditions and a clinician’s assessment. No single inhaler plan is right for every child, adolescent or adult.²\nThis is especially important in children. Preschool wheeze can be linked to viral infections and may not behave like asthma in an older child. A cough may have several causes, and a response to a reliever does not prove a diagnosis. For school-age children and teenagers, clinicians consider symptom patterns, lung-function testing when feasible, growth and development, and ability to use a device. Guidance for adults cannot simply be copied onto a child. Parents, carers, schools and young people share the work of noticing change and using the plan safely.\nThe phrase “stop using the blue inhaler” has travelled faster than its nuance. It should not mean throwing away a prescribed reliever or refusing it during acute symptoms. A reliever remains relevant when a child has the symptoms for which it was prescribed, and an asthma action plan should say how the family’s team wants symptoms handled. The point is to avoid a reliever-only strategy that masks symptoms without addressing risk. Treatment changes belong with the clinician; parents should not independently change medicines based on this article.\nA written asthma action plan turns that principle into something usable. It can describe the child’s usual pattern, early signs of worsening, prescribed medicines, and when to contact the practice, asthma team or emergency service. It is individual, not a universal dosing chart. After a change, follow-up matters: clinicians can review response, technique, adherence, access and side effects, and adjust the plan if benefit is not appearing. A review is a conversation, not a test of perfect compliance.\nTechnique deserves patience. Pressing a metered-dose inhaler and breathing at the right time is learned skill, and children’s coordination changes with age. A spacer, or valved holding chamber, can make delivery easier with some inhalers, particularly for younger children, but the device must match the prescription and be used correctly. The child’s clinician, pharmacist or asthma nurse should watch a demonstration. A device that is unavailable, uncomfortable or unsuitable is a practical problem to solve, not a moral failing.\nThe same non-judgment applies to access and adherence. Prescriptions can be delayed, supplies run out, insurance or transport interfere, and an anti-inflammatory inhaler can cause effects or feel difficult to tolerate. If recommended treatment cannot be obtained, replaced, tolerated or used correctly, contact the prescribing or asthma team promptly for an alternative or review. Do not silently revert to an unsupported rescue-only plan, and do not invent a substitute schedule. If symptoms are severe, emergency care takes priority over finding the perfect device.\nFamilies may also be asked about smoke, vaping aerosol, mould, dust, pollen, pets, cold air or exercise. Reducing exposure where practical can support control, as can treating nasal allergies and keeping vaccinations up to date when advised. Trigger work is supportive care; it does not replace prescribed anti-inflammatory treatment, and avoiding every possible trigger is neither realistic nor necessary. The aim is a workable life with fewer avoidable irritants.\nThere are times when the right next step is reassessment rather than automatic escalation. Symptoms may be changing, the diagnosis uncertain, or testing incomplete. Not every cough or wheeze is asthma, and breathlessness can have other causes. A clinician may check the history, listen to the chest, review measurements and consider alternatives before deciding whether and how to change inhalers. That pause protects children from undertreatment and unnecessary medicine.\nInhaled corticosteroids are not risk-free, but their benefits and risks need context: the prescribed dose, duration, age, growth, technique and the child’s risk from uncontrolled asthma all matter. Discuss concerns about hoarseness, oral thrush or possible growth effects with the clinical team; do not stop suddenly on the basis of a frightening headline. ICS reduce risk; they do not eliminate every exacerbation or make emergency care unnecessary.³\nRoutine symptoms and an asthma attack are different situations. Worsening breathlessness, difficulty speaking in full sentences, marked exhaustion, a blue or grey tinge around the lips, severe chest tightness, or little response to the prescribed reliever are warning signs for urgent help. Follow the child’s action plan and local emergency advice. A family should never delay emergency care while trying to obtain, perfect or negotiate a preferred inhaler regimen.\nThe reason the conversation feels new is that medicine keeps learning. Trials and guideline reviews have shown that pairing symptom relief with treatment aimed at inflammation can reduce serious risk compared with reliever-only care for many patients.⁴ The finding does not erase the usefulness of a blue inhaler or turn every past decision into a mistake. It gives families a better question: what protects this child between episodes as well as helping during one?\nThat question invites partnership. Bring the inhalers to an appointment, ask for a technique check, tell the team when a prescription is hard to obtain, and make sure school or childcare knows the agreed plan. Ask what improvement should look like and when review is due. These are not demands for a perfect household; they are ways to make evidence fit ordinary lives.\nThe blue inhaler can remain a trusted tool without being asked to carry the whole burden of asthma care. Relief matters in the moment. Protection matters in the days when a child feels fine. A calm, individualized plan makes room for both—and leaves families with something more useful than an absolute instruction: a clear understanding of why care is changing, what remains dependable, and when to seek help.\nSources\n1. Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA), Global Strategy for Asthma Management and Prevention, 2024.\n2. National Asthma Education and Prevention Program Coordinating Committee, 2020 Focused Updates to the Asthma Management Guidelines, 2020.\n3. British Thoracic Society and Scottish Intercollegiate Guidelines Network, British guideline on the management of asthma, 2019.\n4. Beasley R, et al., “Controlled Trial of Budesonide-Formoterol as Needed for Mild Asthma,” New England Journal of Medicine, 2019.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"r01","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The feature, including the Sources note, is between 1,300 and 1,400 words inclusive.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Word count from the submitted DOCX text, including the Sources note.","grader_hint":"Extract document text and count words using a consistent whitespace-based method; accept any count from 1300 through 1400."},{"rubric_item_id":"r02","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted file is a readable DOCX named rushmere_asthma_inhaler_feature.docx.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Filename and successful DOCX parsing.","grader_hint":"Check the exact filename and open the document with a standard DOCX parser."},{"rubric_item_id":"r03","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article has a recognizable magazine feature arc: engaging opening, developed middle argument, and deliberate ending.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole-document editorial reading.","grader_hint":"Judge whether the opening creates reader interest, the middle develops rather than repeats the thesis, and the ending provides closure rather than an abrupt instruction list."},{"rubric_item_id":"r04","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The prose sounds like a polished health-foundation magazine feature rather than a handout, lecture, clinical note, or policy memo.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Tone, pacing, paragraphing, and reader address.","grader_hint":"A technically correct but list-like or classroom-like document should not receive full credit."},{"rubric_item_id":"r05","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article identifies the familiar blue rescue inhaler as a short-acting bronchodilator or reliever and explains that symptom relief does not itself treat airway inflammation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Clinical explanation in the feature.","grader_hint":"Look for both the reliever function and the distinction between opening airways and treating inflammation."},{"rubric_item_id":"r06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article explains that asthma involves airway inflammation and variable narrowing or obstruction, using language understandable to nonclinicians.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definition or explanatory passage.","grader_hint":"Require more than the isolated word 'inflammation'; the relationship to changing symptoms or narrowed airways must be clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"r07","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article explains why frequent reliance on a reliever can signal inadequate control or increased risk rather than simply proving that the inhaler is working well.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Discussion of reliever frequency and clinical meaning.","grader_hint":"Accept clinically cautious wording that presents frequent use as a warning sign requiring review, not as an automatic diagnosis."},{"rubric_item_id":"r08","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The feature explains the preventive role of inhaled corticosteroid-containing treatment or another anti-inflammatory strategy without presenting it as an instant substitute for every episode of breathlessness.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Treatment-class explanation.","grader_hint":"The article must distinguish prevention or reduction of exacerbation risk from immediate symptom relief."},{"rubric_item_id":"r09","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The feature describes the broad rationale for pairing anti-inflammatory treatment with relief treatment, including reducing the risk left unaddressed by reliever-only treatment.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Central argument.","grader_hint":"Look for a causal explanation, not merely a statement that guidelines changed."},{"rubric_item_id":"r10","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article states that treatment choices depend on age, symptoms, exacerbation history, diagnosis, and clinician assessment rather than one universal inhaler plan.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope and individualization language.","grader_hint":"At least several relevant clinical factors must be named, and the article must explicitly reject a one-size-fits-all implication."},{"rubric_item_id":"r11","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article distinguishes children or adolescents from adults where age changes the relevance of guidance or treatment decisions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Pediatric framing.","grader_hint":"The feature should not discuss pediatric asthma as though all recommendations were written for adults."},{"rubric_item_id":"r12","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article makes clear that a parent should not independently stop, start, or change a child's prescribed asthma treatment based on the article alone.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Safety limitation.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent wording directing treatment changes to the child's clinician without supplying a rigid script."},{"rubric_item_id":"r13","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article explains that a reliever inhaler remains relevant for acute symptoms even when the overall strategy moves away from reliever-only treatment.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Clarification of the blue-inhaler misconception.","grader_hint":"The document must not leave readers with the impression that all rescue inhalers should simply disappear."},{"rubric_item_id":"r14","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article says that worsening or severe symptoms require prompt clinical assessment and identifies emergency warning signs in plain language.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Urgency and escalation passage.","grader_hint":"Look for examples such as severe difficulty breathing, inability to speak normally, marked exhaustion, blue or gray coloring, or poor response to the prescribed reliever; do not require an exhaustive list."},{"rubric_item_id":"r15","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article states that a written asthma action plan or equivalent individualized plan helps families know what to do when symptoms change.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Self-management support.","grader_hint":"The plan should be described as individualized and linked to escalation, not as a universal dosing chart."},{"rubric_item_id":"r16","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article addresses inhaler technique and the role of a spacer or valved holding chamber where appropriate.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Practical safety content.","grader_hint":"Require both technique and spacer/holding-chamber relevance, while allowing age-appropriate qualification."},{"rubric_item_id":"r17","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The feature mentions adherence, access, or both as reasons a treatment plan may fail in real life and frames these as issues to discuss rather than moral failings.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Real-world implementation discussion.","grader_hint":"Full credit requires a nonjudgmental treatment of practical barriers."},{"rubric_item_id":"r18","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article addresses what families should do when the recommended anti-inflammatory inhaler or device cannot be obtained, tolerated, or used correctly: seek prompt contact with the prescribing or asthma team for an alternative or review rather than silently reverting to an unsupported plan.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Uncompleted-path edge case.","grader_hint":"The feature must provide a safe route when the intended plan cannot be completed, without inventing a replacement dose or regimen."},{"rubric_item_id":"r19","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article acknowledges that an uncertain diagnosis, changing symptoms, or an incomplete assessment may require reassessment rather than automatic escalation of medication.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Diagnostic uncertainty passage.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit recognition that not every cough or wheeze establishes asthma and that assessment matters."},{"rubric_item_id":"r20","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article discusses environmental or trigger management as supportive care without implying that trigger avoidance replaces prescribed anti-inflammatory treatment.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Trigger discussion.","grader_hint":"Accept examples such as smoke exposure, allergens, viral illness, or exercise if framed appropriately."},{"rubric_item_id":"r21","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article explains that guideline changes reflect accumulating evidence and changing understanding of risk, not proof that earlier clinicians or families acted negligently.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Historical and ethical framing.","grader_hint":"Require both the evidence-based change and the non-blaming interpretation."},{"rubric_item_id":"r22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article treats families who have used a blue inhaler for many years with respect and avoids shaming, alarmism, or condescension.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Editorial reading of language and framing.","grader_hint":"Penalize rhetorically blaming parents, patients, or previous clinicians even if the clinical facts are correct."},{"rubric_item_id":"r23","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The feature explains that symptom improvement after a reliever can coexist with ongoing disease risk and should not be used alone to judge control.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk-versus-symptom distinction.","grader_hint":"Require an explicit distinction between feeling better now and reducing future risk."},{"rubric_item_id":"r24","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article describes follow-up or review as important after a treatment change, with attention to response, technique, adherence, and side effects.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Monitoring discussion.","grader_hint":"At least three of the listed review dimensions should appear."},{"rubric_item_id":"r25","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article gives a careful, non-alarmist explanation of potential inhaled-corticosteroid concerns and states that benefits and risks should be discussed in the context of the prescribed dose and clinical need.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Medication-safety discussion.","grader_hint":"Look for balanced discussion rather than either dismissing concerns or presenting common fears as settled harm."},{"rubric_item_id":"r26","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article does not claim that inhaled corticosteroids eliminate all exacerbations or make emergency care unnecessary.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Absence of unsupported absolute claims, assessed in context.","grader_hint":"Credit only if the article preserves residual risk and the need for escalation."},{"rubric_item_id":"r27","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article does not claim that the blue inhaler is inherently dangerous, useless, or forbidden in every circumstance.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Medication characterization.","grader_hint":"Check the overall wording, not just whether one sentence contains the word 'not.'"},{"rubric_item_id":"r28","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article does not claim that every child with wheeze has asthma or that a response to a reliever proves the diagnosis.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Diagnostic restraint.","grader_hint":"Search for categorical diagnostic statements and assess whether the document explicitly preserves uncertainty."},{"rubric_item_id":"r29","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article does not present a specific dose, device schedule, step-up or step-down instruction as universally applicable to readers.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Absence of individualized prescribing.","grader_hint":"General explanation of treatment steps is acceptable only when clearly tied to clinician assessment and not offered as a reader protocol."},{"rubric_item_id":"r30","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article does not suggest that a child should delay emergency care while trying to obtain or perfect a preferred inhaler regimen.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Safety sequencing.","grader_hint":"Ensure urgent symptoms are directed to urgent care independently of routine medication access."},{"rubric_item_id":"r31","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article identifies at least one recognized asthma guideline or evidence source and attributes the relevant change to that source rather than to Rushmere Trust alone.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cited clinical source in prose and Sources note.","grader_hint":"Accept recognized sources such as GINA, NHLBI/NAEPP, BTS/SIGN, or a named peer-reviewed study, provided the attribution is plausible and specific."},{"rubric_item_id":"r32","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every numbered citation used in the prose has a corresponding entry in the Sources note.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Citation cross-check.","grader_hint":"Compare citation numbers in the body with source entries; do not require a particular numbering sequence beyond internal consistency."},{"rubric_item_id":"r33","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Each Sources note entry provides an organization or author, a source title, and a year.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Sources note metadata.","grader_hint":"Check each cited entry for all three required elements."},{"rubric_item_id":"r34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Attributions are proportionate to a magazine feature: credible and readable without turning the article into a dense academic reference list.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Editorial reading of citation placement and Sources note.","grader_hint":"Full credit requires both traceability and reasonable readability."},{"rubric_item_id":"r35","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article uses plain-language explanations or brief definitions for technical terms such as reliever, controller, anti-inflammatory, exacerbation, or airway inflammation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Reader-facing terminology.","grader_hint":"A technical term may remain if its meaning is made clear in context."},{"rubric_item_id":"r36","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article keeps the reader's central question visible: why a familiar rescue-only approach is being reconsidered now.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole-document coherence.","grader_hint":"The explanation should develop a clear answer rather than become a general asthma overview."},{"rubric_item_id":"r37","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article distinguishes routine symptom management from an asthma attack or severe exacerbation requiring urgent attention.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Clinical safety distinction.","grader_hint":"Require an explicit difference in severity or response, not merely separate headings."},{"rubric_item_id":"r38","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article acknowledges that children may need support from parents, caregivers, schools, or other adults in recognizing symptoms and using treatment safely.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Pediatric practical context.","grader_hint":"The article should recognize shared responsibility without exposing any individual child."},{"rubric_item_id":"r39","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The feature explains that device choice and technique can affect delivery and that the child's clinician should check whether the selected device is suitable.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Device-use guidance.","grader_hint":"Look for suitability and technique, not a universal device recommendation."},{"rubric_item_id":"r40","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document contains no identifiable patient story, patient name, address, medical record number, or other unnecessary personal health information.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Document-wide privacy review.","grader_hint":"Generic composite examples are acceptable only if clearly non-identifying and not presented as a real patient."},{"rubric_item_id":"r41","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The article does not invent local prevalence figures, Rushmere Trust outcomes, named patient quotations, or unsupported claims about what most families do.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Factual and sourcing review.","grader_hint":"Check numerical and anecdotal claims against the absence of supplied local data; general evidence-based statements may remain."},{"rubric_item_id":"r42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article resolves the tension between reassuring readers about the older approach and clearly communicating why the newer approach addresses risk differently.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Editorial and clinical synthesis.","grader_hint":"Full credit requires an explicit, credible trade-off rather than either bland reassurance or accusatory correction."},{"rubric_item_id":"r43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article handles the edge case of unavailable, intolerable, or unusable recommended treatment without pretending the standard plan can always be completed.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Discussion of access, tolerance, technique, and follow-up.","grader_hint":"Look for a safe and actionable clinical-contact pathway while preserving the boundary against self-prescribing."},{"rubric_item_id":"r44","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The article is technically correct yet usable for a general reader: claims are qualified where necessary, important distinctions are visible, and no paragraph requires a clinician to decode it before publication.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Independent clinical-editorial review.","grader_hint":"This is a holistic usability judgment; award full credit only when accuracy and accessibility coexist."},{"rubric_item_id":"r45","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document contains no title page, author biography, editor's note, pull-quote block, or separate reference attachment and is presented as one continuous feature with its Sources note.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Document structure inspection.","grader_hint":"A headline and subhead within the feature are acceptable; disallowed front matter or detached appendices are not."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"A nurse practitioner writing for a large health-foundation magazine must turn clinical knowledge about changing asthma-reliever guidance into a trustworthy public-facing feature. The document will be reviewed by a clinical peer and a magazine editor for accuracy, safety, voice, and publishability.","difficulty_sources":["Translate current professional asthma-management principles into accurate, accessible magazine prose without turning the feature into a handout.","Explain a change in practice without blaming patients or earlier clinicians.","Balance a strict word limit with safety-critical distinctions and appropriate uncertainty.","Handle the edge case in which the preferred anti-inflammatory reliever or controller cannot be obtained or used as intended.","Use a consistent, reader-facing attribution style without supplied source files."],"authoring_plan":{"reference_files":[],"source_strategy":"The build agent should create no reference files. The gold should be a self-contained DOCX feature grounded in stable occupational knowledge about asthma, pediatric medication safety, inhaler technique, anti-inflammatory treatment, reliever use, action plans, and urgent escalation. It may cite recognized guideline bodies or peer-reviewed evidence using the convention stated in the request.","gold_content_requirements":["Write a magazine feature with an engaging lede, a clear thesis, explanatory development, and a concluding passage.","Explain the distinction between rapid bronchodilation and treatment of airway inflammation, and explain why reliever-only treatment is being reconsidered.","Preserve the relevance of reliever inhalers for acute symptoms while avoiding the absolute message that families should discard or universally stop them.","Cover pediatric variation, diagnostic uncertainty, treatment review, inhaler technique, spacer or holding-chamber use, adherence, access, trigger support, and individualized action plans.","Include a clear safety route for a family who cannot obtain, tolerate, replace, or correctly use the recommended anti-inflammatory treatment: contact the clinical team promptly, seek urgent care when severe symptoms occur, and do not invent a substitute regimen.","Use non-blaming language toward families and clinicians who followed older practice.","Avoid patient identifiers, invented local statistics, individualized doses, universal schedules, and categorical claims that all wheeze is asthma or that any inhaler makes emergency care unnecessary.","Use numbered superscript citations in the body and a compact Sources note with matching entries containing author or organization, title, and year.","Keep the complete document between 1,300 and 1,400 words and omit all disallowed front matter."],"derivation_rules":{"clinical_claims":"The central explanation should be supported by recognized asthma guidance or evidence and should distinguish symptom relief, prevention, control, exacerbation risk, and emergency escalation.","doctrinal_boundaries":"For every positive treatment principle, preserve its boundary: anti-inflammatory treatment is not a universal emergency substitute; reliever use is not universally forbidden; guideline change does not prove prior negligence; an article cannot prescribe for an individual child; and lack of access cannot be treated as successful completion of a plan.","tension_one":"Create the explicit trade-off between reassuring readers that earlier rescue-only practice was understandable in its historical context and clearly communicating that evidence and risk framing now support a different strategy.","tension_two":"Create the explicit trade-off between fitting necessary safety nuance into a readable 1,400-word magazine feature and avoiding an oversimplified slogan about stopping the blue inhaler.","edge_case":"Include a family for whom the recommended treatment is unavailable, not tolerated, not correctly usable, or not yet appropriate because assessment is incomplete. The article must give a clinically safe route without supplying an invented replacement prescription.","citation_consistency":"Derive the gold's Sources note directly from the numbered citations used in the body; no unmatched citation numbers or unexplained source list entries."},"review_pass":"Before marking the reference solution passed, count the words, parse and render the DOCX, verify every citation-to-source mapping, and have the two reviewers independently walk all 45 criteria. Confirm that no criterion depends on a private code or unavailable supplied source."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"primary_plus_appendix","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"magazine_feature","filename":"rushmere_asthma_inhaler_feature.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A publishable magazine feature explaining the move away from rescue-only asthma treatment, with numbered superscript citations and a compact Sources note.","required":true,"constraints":["Use exactly the specified filename and place it at the top level of the working directory.","The feature, including the Sources note, must be 1,300 to 1,400 words inclusive.","Do not include a title page, author biography, editor's note, pull quotes, patient-identifying details, or individualized prescribing instructions.","Use numbered superscript citations in the prose and a Sources note identifying cited sources by organization or author, title, and year.","The prose must be suitable for publication in a general-interest health magazine and must not read like a clinical handout."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"document_information","work_product":"clinical_administrative_package","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"nurse_practitioners","sector":"healthcare_social_assistance","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":4.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Have one pediatric asthma subject-matter reviewer assess clinical accuracy and doctrinal restraint, and one health-magazine editor assess feature structure, voice, attribution, word count, and publication usability. Score relationally against the criteria and accept equivalent clinically sound wording.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No supplied guideline files are available, so source-specific wording and publication years must be checked for internal plausibility and professional accuracy rather than against a fixed local source list.","Word counts can vary slightly by tokenization method; use one documented counting method consistently."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:46:32+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:46:32+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:46:32+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:46:32+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":false,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"instruction 与 rubric criterion 存在 8-gram 逐字重叠: ['r01', 'r45']","checked_at":"2026-08-20T18:46:32+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/e75b0b93211820a9","declared_deliverables":["rushmere_asthma_inhaler_feature.docx"]};
